From 56829bc6273480d1038949c29ac2a63b0319a4b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jky72 <jky72@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 09:11:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] fix(render): stop a minimized window from killing or
 mis-sizing the child

Minimizing killed the process with an `f32::clamp` whose min exceeded its max, and
the stock 0.45.0 binary dies the same way, so this was never a chrome-patch
regression. The numbers in the panic (min = 0.0, max = -19.0) are the scrollbar's
thumb arithmetic: the thumb height was raised to a 20px clickable minimum while the
track collapses to about a pixel when the window is icon-sized, so the travel range
went negative. Clamp the thumb to the track, hide the scrollbar when the track cannot
hold it, and skip frame construction while minimized - a minimized window has no
drawable area, which is the other half of that clamp path.

Two more ways a bogus one-row grid still reached the children, both found by watching
what the child actually received rather than what par-term logged:

* The settling gate promoted the grid it had remembered from before the minimize. A
  minimized window reports its icon size (192x34 -> 13x1) and frames resume a few
  milliseconds before the restored size event arrives, so the same stale grid was seen
  on two "consecutive" frames. Record that the grid is untrustworthy from the moment
  the window is minimized until a real Resized event clears it, and hold the children
  on their last good size in the meantime. The failure direction is safe: the worst
  case is a child that keeps the size it already had.
* gather_pane_render_data resizes the PTY on every frame and bypassed that gate
  entirely, which is why the one-row grid survived the first fix. Give it the same
  trust flag, skipping only the commit so the viewport still matches the surface.
  clippy::too_many_arguments is allowed there, as elsewhere in this tree, because the
  parameters come from four different owners.

The two per-frame paths are now both gated. The remaining resize call sites are
one-shot user actions (open/close a tab, change a setting) with no relation to
minimizing.

Also send a submitted command with CR on Windows: ConPTY takes a bare LF as a literal
newline in the input buffer rather than as Enter, so a command sent with \n was typed
into the prompt and never run.
